"""Full pages behind the home page's "Did you know?" facts.

Ifan, 12 November 2026: "things like this need to be clickable to a page full of information". Every fact in
features.FACTS now links to a page (its third field). Where no full page existed, it is written here:

- LONG: full text for existing place pages (merged into long.PLACE_LONG): the Great Orme mines.
- PLACES: new place pages (added to data.PLACES): Llanfairpwll, Bethlehem, Pen-y-Gwryd, Lavernock.

Text uses the prose codes of build.prose(): "## heading", "|| left | right" table rows, "> quote | source".
Sources sit beside the claims with [[cite:key]] markers (src/cites.py). Every fact here was checked against the
source named beside it, November 2026.
"""


def _p(id, en, cy, era, lat, lon, year, ill, src, dEn, dCy):
    return dict(id=id, en=en, cy=cy, era=era, lat=lat, lon=lon, year=year, ill=ill, src=src, dEn=dEn, dCy=dCy)


OA = "[[cite:orme-antiquity]]"

LONG = {
 "orme": ([
  "High on the Great Orme above Llandudno, Bronze Age miners dug for copper nearly four thousand years ago. With hammers of beach stone and picks of animal bone they followed the green ore deep into the limestone, and left behind kilometres of tunnels and a great cavern hollowed out of the rock. The Great Orme is among the largest prehistoric mines yet found anywhere in the world.[[cite:orme-gsl,orme-guinness,orme-visitwales]] For two hundred years its copper fed the bronze of Britain, and it was carried over the sea to Ireland, Brittany, the Low Countries and Sweden." + OA,
  "|| Where | Pen y Gogarth, the Great Orme, above Llandudno",
  "|| Worked | About 1700 to 900 BC; the richest years about 1600 to 1400 BC",
  "|| The ore | Malachite, a soft green copper ore, in veins of dolomite",
  "|| The tools | More than 2,400 stone hammers and over 30,000 pieces of bone",
  "|| The workings | Several kilometres of tunnels, and a great cavern 8 metres high",
  "|| Copper made | An estimated 232 to 830 tonnes of metal",
  "|| Found again | 1987",
  "|| Visit | The Great Orme Mines, open mid-March to the end of October",
  "## Found again in 1987",
  "For three thousand years the old workings lay hidden. Copper was mined on the Orme again from 1692 to 1881, but the far older workings were forgotten.[[cite:orme-guinness]] Then in 1987 a landscaping scheme on the headland broke into an extraordinary complex of surface and underground workings.[[cite:orme-antiquity,orme-subbrit]] Inside lay stone hammers and bone tools, and radiocarbon dating showed that the tunnels were the work of the Bronze Age.",
  "## How the miners worked",
  "The Orme is a headland of Carboniferous limestone. Through it run veins where the rock has turned to dolomite, and in them the copper ore chalcopyrite had weathered over the ages into malachite and goethite. These were soft enough to dig out and far easier to smelt than the hard ore beneath, and the miners followed them wherever they led." + OA,
  "They worked with what the land gave them. Rounded cobbles from the beaches served as hammers to break the rock, and the bones of cattle and other animals as picks and scrapers to dig out the soft ore. Archaeologists have found more than 2,400 stone hammers and more than 30,000 pieces of bone, many of them worn from use. Fragments of bronze tools have been found too: the miners were digging the metal that made them." + OA,
  "## The great cavern",
  "At the heart of the mine, beneath the opencast, the miners hollowed out a chamber some 10 by 15 metres across and 8 metres high. Around it run several kilometres of smaller passages, most of them low and twisting, following the narrow veins." + OA + " Visit Wales calls the Great Cavern the largest prehistoric underground excavation in the world.[[cite:orme-visitwales]] To make it all, the miners moved at least 16,000 tonnes of rock, and perhaps as much as 40,000." + OA,
  "## Britain’s first mining boom",
  "Nineteen radiocarbon dates show that the mine was worked for about eight centuries, from around 1700 to 900 BC. Its golden age came between about 1600 and 1400 BC, when the two richest bodies of ore were being dug and the Orme was the great source of copper in Britain. The archaeologists who dated it call this Britain’s first mining boom.[[cite:orme-antiquity,orme-liverpool]]",
  "Over its life the mine is thought to have given between 232 and 830 tonnes of copper, most of it in those two richest centuries: in its best years enough metal for thousands of bronze axes a year. Work on that scale was not done by farmers in a spare season. The people who worked the Orme were most likely a mining community, living close by and working the mine for most of the year, who traded their metal for food, perhaps with the rich farmland of north-east Wales." + OA,
  "## Orme copper across Europe",
  "Copper from each ore body carries its own chemical fingerprint, in the traces of other metals and in the make-up of its lead. By matching these, archaeologists have traced Orme metal in the bronze axes of Wales and England, above all the shield-pattern palstaves found thick on the ground in north-east Wales and the borderland. They have traced it overseas as well: in Ireland, in the Tréboul hoard in Brittany, in the Voorhout hoard in the Netherlands, and in hoards in western Sweden." + OA + " Three and a half thousand years ago, copper dug from a headland in Gwynedd was being made into tools and weapons across north-west Europe.",
  "## The long twilight",
  "By about 1400 BC the richest ore was worked out. Only narrow, poor veins were left, which gave little for a great deal of labour, and the mine settled into a long twilight of small-scale working that went on for centuries. Ore was still being smelted at Pentrwyn on the Orme in about 900 BC." + OA,
  "## Visiting the mines",
  "The Great Orme Mines are open to visitors from mid-March to the end of October. A self-guided tour leads through some 200 metres of Bronze Age passages to the Great Cavern. Above ground are the opencast, a smelting shelter and a visitor centre that tells the story of the rediscovery, and visitors can look down a 470-foot shaft from the nineteenth-century mine.[[cite:orme-visitwales,orme-subbrit]] It is the oldest metal mine open to the public anywhere in the world.[[cite:orme-visitwales]]",
  "The headland has older stories still. In Kendrick’s Cave on the Orme, Ice Age people decorated a horse’s jaw with zigzag lines, and Saint Tudno, who gave Llandudno its name, built his church on the hill.",
 ], [
  "Yn uchel ar y Gogarth uwchben Llandudno, bu mwynwyr Oes yr Efydd yn cloddio am gopr bron i bedair mil o flynyddoedd yn ôl. Â morthwylion o gerrig y traeth a cheibiau o esgyrn anifeiliaid dilynasant y mwyn gwyrdd yn ddwfn i’r calchfaen, gan adael ar eu hôl gilometrau o dwneli ac ogof fawr wedi’i chafnu o’r graig. Mae’r Gogarth ymhlith y mwyngloddiau cynhanesyddol mwyaf a ddarganfuwyd yn unman yn y byd.[[cite:orme-gsl,orme-guinness,orme-visitwales]] Am ddau gan mlynedd bu ei gopr yn bwydo efydd Prydain, a chariwyd ef dros y môr i Iwerddon, Llydaw, y Gwledydd Isel a Sweden." + OA,
  "|| Ble | Pen y Gogarth, uwchben Llandudno",
  "|| Pryd | Tua 1700 i 900 CC; y blynyddoedd cyfoethocaf tua 1600 i 1400 CC",
  "|| Y mwyn | Malacit, mwyn copr gwyrdd meddal, mewn gwythiennau o ddolomit",
  "|| Yr offer | Dros 2,400 o forthwylion carreg a dros 30,000 o ddarnau o asgwrn",
  "|| Y gweithfeydd | Sawl cilometr o dwneli, ac ogof fawr 8 metr o uchder",
  "|| Copr a gafwyd | Rhwng tua 232 ac 830 tunnell o fetel",
  "|| Ailddarganfod | 1987",
  "|| Ymweld | Mwyngloddiau’r Gogarth, ar agor o ganol Mawrth hyd ddiwedd Hydref",
  "## Eu darganfod eto yn 1987",
  "Am dair mil o flynyddoedd bu’r hen weithfeydd yn guddiedig. Cloddiwyd copr ar y Gogarth eto rhwng 1692 ac 1881, ond roedd y gweithfeydd llawer hŷn wedi mynd yn angof.[[cite:orme-guinness]] Yna yn 1987 torrodd cynllun tirlunio ar y pentir i mewn i gyfundrefn ryfeddol o weithfeydd ar yr wyneb a than ddaear.[[cite:orme-antiquity,orme-subbrit]] Y tu mewn roedd morthwylion carreg ac offer asgwrn, a dangosodd dyddio radiocarbon mai gwaith Oes yr Efydd oedd y twneli.",
  "## Sut y gweithiai’r mwynwyr",
  "Pentir o galchfaen Carbonifferaidd yw’r Gogarth. Drwyddo rhed gwythiennau lle trodd y graig yn ddolomit, ac ynddynt roedd y mwyn copr calcopyrit wedi hindreulio dros yr oesoedd yn falacit a goethit. Roedd y rhain yn ddigon meddal i’w cloddio ac yn llawer haws eu toddi na’r mwyn caled oddi tanynt, a dilynai’r mwynwyr hwy i ble bynnag yr arweinient." + OA,
  "Gweithient â’r hyn a roddai’r tir iddynt. Defnyddid cerrig crwn o’r traethau fel morthwylion i dorri’r graig, ac esgyrn gwartheg ac anifeiliaid eraill fel ceibiau a chrafwyr i godi’r mwyn meddal. Mae archaeolegwyr wedi dod o hyd i fwy na 2,400 o forthwylion carreg a mwy na 30,000 o ddarnau o asgwrn, llawer ohonynt wedi’u treulio gan waith. Cafwyd darnau o offer efydd hefyd: roedd y mwynwyr yn cloddio’r metel a’u gwnaeth." + OA,
  "## Yr ogof fawr",
  "Yng nghanol y mwynglawdd, o dan y cloddfa agored, cafnodd y mwynwyr siambr tua 10 wrth 15 metr ac 8 metr o uchder. O’i chwmpas rhed sawl cilometr o dramwyfeydd llai, y rhan fwyaf yn isel ac yn droellog, yn dilyn y gwythiennau cul." + OA + " Mae Croeso Cymru yn galw’r Ogof Fawr y cloddiad tanddaearol cynhanesyddol mwyaf yn y byd.[[cite:orme-visitwales]] I wneud y cyfan symudodd y mwynwyr o leiaf 16,000 tunnell o graig, a hyd at 40,000 efallai." + OA,
  "## Ffyniant mwyngloddio cyntaf Prydain",
  "Mae pedwar ar bymtheg o ddyddiadau radiocarbon yn dangos i’r mwynglawdd gael ei weithio am tua wyth canrif, o tua 1700 i 900 CC. Daeth ei oes aur rhwng tua 1600 a 1400 CC, pan oedd y ddau gorff cyfoethocaf o fwyn yn cael eu cloddio a’r Gogarth oedd prif ffynhonnell copr Prydain. Mae’r archaeolegwyr a’i dyddiodd yn galw hyn yn ffyniant mwyngloddio cyntaf Prydain.[[cite:orme-antiquity,orme-liverpool]]",
  "Dros ei oes credir i’r mwynglawdd roi rhwng 232 ac 830 tunnell o gopr, y rhan fwyaf yn y ddwy ganrif gyfoethocaf hynny: yn ei flynyddoedd gorau, digon o fetel i filoedd o fwyeill efydd y flwyddyn. Nid ffermwyr yn eu hamser hamdden a wnâi waith ar y raddfa honno. Mae’n fwyaf tebyg mai cymuned o fwynwyr a weithiai’r Gogarth, yn byw gerllaw ac yn gweithio’r mwynglawdd am y rhan fwyaf o’r flwyddyn, gan gyfnewid eu metel am fwyd, efallai â thiroedd ffrwythlon gogledd-ddwyrain Cymru." + OA,
  "## Copr y Gogarth ar draws Ewrop",
  "Mae copr o bob corff o fwyn yn cario ei olion bysedd cemegol ei hun, yn olion metelau eraill ac yng ngwneuthuriad ei blwm. Drwy eu cymharu, mae archaeolegwyr wedi olrhain metel y Gogarth ym mwyeill efydd Cymru a Lloegr, yn enwedig y palstafau patrwm tarian a geir yn drwch yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru a’r gororau. Maent wedi’i olrhain dramor hefyd: yn Iwerddon, yng nghelc Tréboul yn Llydaw, yng nghelc Voorhout yn yr Iseldiroedd, ac mewn celciau yng ngorllewin Sweden." + OA + " Dair mil a hanner o flynyddoedd yn ôl, roedd copr a gloddiwyd o bentir yng Ngwynedd yn cael ei wneud yn offer ac arfau ar draws gogledd-orllewin Ewrop.",
  "## Y cyfnos hir",
  "Erbyn tua 1400 CC roedd y mwyn cyfoethocaf wedi’i weithio allan. Dim ond gwythiennau cul a thlawd oedd ar ôl, a roddai fawr ddim am lawer o lafur, ac aeth y mwynglawdd i gyfnos hir o weithio ar raddfa fach a barhaodd am ganrifoedd. Roedd mwyn yn dal i gael ei doddi ym Mhentrwyn ar y Gogarth tua 900 CC." + OA,
  "## Ymweld â’r mwyngloddiau",
  "Mae Mwyngloddiau’r Gogarth ar agor i ymwelwyr o ganol mis Mawrth hyd ddiwedd mis Hydref. Mae taith hunandywys yn arwain drwy tua 200 metr o dramwyfeydd Oes yr Efydd i’r Ogof Fawr. Uwchben y ddaear mae’r gloddfa agored, cysgodfa doddi a chanolfan ymwelwyr sy’n adrodd hanes yr ailddarganfod, a gall ymwelwyr edrych i lawr siafft 470 troedfedd o’r mwynglawdd yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg.[[cite:orme-visitwales,orme-subbrit]] Dyma’r mwynglawdd metel hynaf ar agor i’r cyhoedd yn unman yn y byd.[[cite:orme-visitwales]]",
  "Mae gan y pentir hanesion hŷn eto. Yn Ogof Kendrick ar y Gogarth, addurnodd pobl Oes yr Iâ ên ceffyl â llinellau igam-ogam, ac adeiladodd Sant Tudno, a roddodd ei enw i Landudno, ei eglwys ar y bryn.",
 ]),

 "llanfairpwll": ([
  "Llanfairpwllgwyngyllgogerychwyrndrobwllllantysiliogogogoch, on Ynys Môn above the Menai Strait, has the longest place name in Europe, and one of the longest in the world. For centuries the village was simply Llanfairpwllgwyngyll. The long name was put together in the 1860s by a local man, to draw visitors and to give its railway station the longest name in Britain.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]] It worked: the station sign is still photographed every day.",
  "|| Where | South-east Ynys Môn, above the Menai Strait",
  "|| The old name | Llanfairpwllgwyngyll",
  "|| The long name | 58 letters, made up in the 1860s",
  "|| Everyday names | Llanfairpwll or Llanfair PG",
  "|| Also | The first Women’s Institute in Britain, 1915",
  "## What the name means",
  "Like most Welsh place names, the name describes the place. Llanfair is the church of Mary; pwll gwyngyll is the hollow of the white hazel; gogerychwyrndrobwll is near the fierce whirlpool; and llantysiliogogogoch is the church of Saint Tysilio by the red cave. Put together: Saint Mary’s church in the hollow of the white hazel, near the fierce whirlpool and the church of Saint Tysilio by the red cave.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]]",
  "The whirlpool is very likely the Swellies, the stretch of the Menai Strait between Telford’s suspension bridge and the Britannia Bridge, where the tides come in from both ends at different times and the water boils over the rocks.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]]",
  "## A name for the railway age",
  "The Chester and Holyhead railway crossed Môn in the middle of the nineteenth century, and the little station at Llanfairpwll was where travellers first came into the island. In the 1860s a resident pushed for the long name, to bring tourists and give the station the longest name in Britain.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]] The village still uses its short names day to day: Llanfairpwll, or just Llanfair PG.",
  "## The first Women’s Institute in Britain",
  "Llanfairpwll has another first. The Women’s Institute in Britain began here, on 16 September 1915, when the women of the village met to found the first branch.[[cite:llanfair-bangor,llanfair-coflein]] They met first in a cottage, and between the wars they moved into the hall they still use, a single-storey building of corrugated iron next to the old tollhouse of about 1823.[[cite:llanfair-coflein]] From that one branch grew a movement of hundreds of thousands of women.",
 ], [
  "Llanfairpwllgwyngyllgogerychwyrndrobwllllantysiliogogogoch, ar Ynys Môn uwchben Afon Menai, sydd â’r enw lle hiraf yn Ewrop, ac un o’r hiraf yn y byd. Am ganrifoedd Llanfairpwllgwyngyll yn unig oedd y pentref. Lluniwyd yr enw hir yn y 1860au gan ŵr lleol, i ddenu ymwelwyr ac i roi i’r orsaf reilffordd yr enw hiraf ym Mhrydain.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]] Fe weithiodd: mae arwydd yr orsaf yn cael ei ffotograffu bob dydd hyd heddiw.",
  "|| Ble | De-ddwyrain Ynys Môn, uwchben Afon Menai",
  "|| Yr hen enw | Llanfairpwllgwyngyll",
  "|| Yr enw hir | 58 llythyren, a luniwyd yn y 1860au",
  "|| Enwau bob dydd | Llanfairpwll neu Llanfair PG",
  "|| Hefyd | Sefydliad y Merched cyntaf ym Mhrydain, 1915",
  "## Ystyr yr enw",
  "Fel y rhan fwyaf o enwau lleoedd Cymru, mae’r enw’n disgrifio’r lle. Llanfair yw eglwys Fair; pwll gwyngyll yw pant y gollen wen; gogerychwyrndrobwll yw ger y trobwll chwyrn; a llantysiliogogogoch yw eglwys Sant Tysilio ger yr ogof goch. Gyda’i gilydd: eglwys y Santes Fair ym mhant y gollen wen ger y trobwll chwyrn ac eglwys Sant Tysilio ger yr ogof goch.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]]",
  "Mae’n debyg mai’r Pwll Ceris yw’r trobwll, y darn o Afon Menai rhwng pont grog Telford a Phont Britannia, lle daw’r llanw i mewn o’r ddau ben ar wahanol adegau a’r dŵr yn berwi dros y creigiau.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]]",
  "## Enw i oes y rheilffordd",
  "Croesodd rheilffordd Caer a Chaergybi Ynys Môn yng nghanol y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg, a’r orsaf fach yn Llanfairpwll oedd y man lle deuai teithwyr i’r ynys gyntaf. Yn y 1860au gwthiodd un o’r trigolion am yr enw hir, i ddenu twristiaid a rhoi i’r orsaf yr enw hiraf ym Mhrydain.[[cite:llanfair-nasa]] Mae’r pentref yn dal i ddefnyddio’i enwau byr bob dydd: Llanfairpwll, neu Llanfair PG.",
  "## Sefydliad y Merched cyntaf ym Mhrydain",
  "Mae gan Lanfairpwll beth cyntaf arall. Yma y dechreuodd Sefydliad y Merched ym Mhrydain, ar 16 Medi 1915, pan ddaeth gwragedd y pentref ynghyd i sefydlu’r gangen gyntaf.[[cite:llanfair-bangor,llanfair-coflein]] Cyfarfuant gyntaf mewn bwthyn, a rhwng y rhyfeloedd symudasant i’r neuadd y maent yn ei defnyddio hyd heddiw, adeilad unllawr o haearn rhychog wrth ymyl yr hen dollty o tua 1823.[[cite:llanfair-coflein]] O’r un gangen honno tyfodd mudiad o gannoedd o filoedd o fenywod.",
 ]),

 "bethlehem": ([
  "Bethlehem is a small farming village in the Tywi valley in Carmarthenshire, between Llandeilo and Llangadog. Every December people come from all over Britain and from abroad to post their Christmas cards here, so that they carry the village’s special Christmas handstamp, with an angel and a harp and a greeting in Welsh and English.[[cite:bethlehem-radio]]",
  "|| Where | The Tywi valley, Carmarthenshire, between Llandeilo and Llangadog",
  "|| Old name | Dyffryn Ceidrych, the valley of the Ceidrych",
  "|| Named after | Its chapel, Bethlehem, in the years of the Methodist revival",
  "|| Above it | Carn Goch, an Iron Age hillfort",
  "|| Famous for | The Bethlehem Christmas handstamp",
  "## A village named after its chapel",
  "The village was once known as Dyffryn Ceidrych, after the stream that runs through it. Its chapel was given the name Bethlehem, the name of Christ’s birthplace as William Morgan wrote it in his Welsh Bible of 1588, and in the years of the Methodist revival the people of the valley came to call the village after its chapel.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Across Wales there are villages that took their names from their chapels in the same way: Bethesda, Nebo, Carmel, Saron and Hebron.",
  "## Christmas cards from Bethlehem",
  "The idea of posting Christmas cards from Bethlehem caught the nation’s attention in the 1960s.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Since then, every December, people have made the journey to the village to have their cards stamped by hand. The handstamp shows an angel and a harp, with the greeting in Welsh and English, and Royal Mail says that thousands of its customers use it.[[cite:bethlehem-radio]] The village holds a traditional Christmas market as well.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]]",
  "## Carn Goch",
  "Above the village rises Carn Goch, an Iron Age hillfort whose great ramparts have tumbled into banks of grey stone that can be seen for miles along the valley.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Long before there was a chapel or a post office, this was one of the strongholds of the people of the Tywi.",
 ], [
  "Pentref ffermio bach yn Nyffryn Tywi yn Sir Gaerfyrddin, rhwng Llandeilo a Llangadog, yw Bethlehem. Bob mis Rhagfyr daw pobl o bob rhan o Brydain a thramor i bostio’u cardiau Nadolig yma, er mwyn iddynt gario stamp llaw Nadolig arbennig y pentref, ag angel a thelyn a chyfarchiad yn Gymraeg ac yn Saesneg.[[cite:bethlehem-radio]]",
  "|| Ble | Dyffryn Tywi, Sir Gaerfyrddin, rhwng Llandeilo a Llangadog",
  "|| Hen enw | Dyffryn Ceidrych",
  "|| Cafodd ei enw gan | Ei gapel, Bethlehem, ym mlynyddoedd y diwygiad Methodistaidd",
  "|| Uwch ei ben | Carn Goch, bryngaer o Oes yr Haearn",
  "|| Yn enwog am | Stamp llaw Nadolig Bethlehem",
  "## Pentref a gafodd ei enw gan ei gapel",
  "Dyffryn Ceidrych oedd enw’r pentref ar un adeg, ar ôl y nant sy’n llifo drwyddo. Rhoddwyd yr enw Bethlehem ar ei gapel, enw man geni Crist fel yr ysgrifennodd William Morgan ef yn ei Feibl Cymraeg yn 1588, ac ym mlynyddoedd y diwygiad Methodistaidd daeth pobl y cwm i alw’r pentref ar ôl ei gapel.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Ar draws Cymru mae pentrefi a gafodd eu henwau gan eu capeli yn yr un modd: Bethesda, Nebo, Carmel, Saron a Hebron.",
  "## Cardiau Nadolig o Fethlehem",
  "Daliodd y syniad o bostio cardiau Nadolig o Fethlehem sylw’r genedl yn y 1960au.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Ers hynny, bob mis Rhagfyr, mae pobl wedi teithio i’r pentref i gael stampio’u cardiau â llaw. Mae’r stamp llaw yn dangos angel a thelyn, gyda’r cyfarchiad yn Gymraeg ac yn Saesneg, ac mae’r Post Brenhinol yn dweud bod miloedd o’i gwsmeriaid yn ei ddefnyddio.[[cite:bethlehem-radio]] Mae’r pentref yn cynnal marchnad Nadolig draddodiadol hefyd.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]]",
  "## Carn Goch",
  "Uwchben y pentref cyfyd Carn Goch, bryngaer o Oes yr Haearn y mae ei rhagfuriau mawr wedi syrthio’n gloddiau o gerrig llwyd sydd i’w gweld am filltiroedd ar hyd y dyffryn.[[cite:bethlehem-wikishire]] Ymhell cyn bod capel na swyddfa bost, dyma un o gadarnleoedd pobl y Tywi.",
 ]),

 "penygwryd": ([
  "The Pen-y-Gwryd Hotel stands at the foot of Yr Wyddfa, where the road from Capel Curig meets the roads to Llanberis and Beddgelert. In 1953 it was home to the British Everest team as they trained and tested their equipment in the mountains of Eryri, and the signatures of the men who climbed Everest are still on the ceiling of its dining room.[[cite:everest-pyg,pyg-wiki]]",
  "|| Where | The head of Nant Gwynant, near Pen-y-Pass, Eryri",
  "|| Built | A farmhouse of 1811, an inn by 1840",
  "|| Everest | Training base of the 1953 expedition",
  "|| On the ceiling | Hillary, Tenzing, Hunt and the Everest and Kangchenjunga teams",
  "|| Also | The Climbers’ Club began here in 1898",
  "## Everest, 1953",
  "When John Hunt gathered the team that would climb Everest, they came to Eryri to train and to test their equipment, the oxygen sets above all, on the rocks and snow of the mountains around Yr Wyddfa, and the Pen-y-Gwryd was their home.[[cite:everest-pyg,pyg-wiki]] On 29 May 1953 Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay stood on the summit of the world.",
  "They did not forget the hotel. In its little dining room, the members of the Everest team, and of the 1955 expedition that first climbed Kangchenjunga, signed their names on the ceiling: Hillary, Tenzing and Hunt among them. The signatures are still there, and the teams came back for reunions for many years.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
  "## A climbers’ inn",
  "The house began as a farmhouse in 1811 and was an inn by 1840. Under Henry and Ann Owen, who kept it for nearly fifty years until the 1890s, it became the best-known climbers’ inn in Wales. In May 1898 the Climbers’ Club was founded here.[[cite:pyg-wiki]] The bedrooms are named after the peaks of Eryri over three thousand feet, and the hotel is still a mountain rescue post.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
  "## Older than the inn",
  "The Romans were here first. At the head of Dyffryn Mymbyr, on the line the road still takes, the army built a marching camp of about four hectares, big enough for some two thousand men, probably during Agricola’s war against the Ordovices in the late 70s AD. Its northern rampart runs under the hotel.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
 ], [
  "Saif Gwesty Pen-y-Gwryd wrth droed yr Wyddfa, lle mae’r ffordd o Gapel Curig yn cwrdd â’r ffyrdd i Lanberis a Beddgelert. Yn 1953 dyma gartref tîm Everest Prydain wrth iddynt ymarfer a phrofi eu hoffer ym mynyddoedd Eryri, ac mae llofnodion y dynion a ddringodd Everest yn dal ar nenfwd ei ystafell fwyta.[[cite:everest-pyg,pyg-wiki]]",
  "|| Ble | Pen Nant Gwynant, ger Pen-y-Pass, Eryri",
  "|| Codwyd | Ffermdy o 1811, tafarn erbyn 1840",
  "|| Everest | Canolfan ymarfer alldaith 1953",
  "|| Ar y nenfwd | Hillary, Tenzing, Hunt a thimau Everest a Kangchenjunga",
  "|| Hefyd | Yma y dechreuodd y Climbers’ Club yn 1898",
  "## Everest, 1953",
  "Pan gasglodd John Hunt y tîm a fyddai’n dringo Everest, daethant i Eryri i ymarfer ac i brofi eu hoffer, yr offer ocsigen yn anad dim, ar greigiau ac eira’r mynyddoedd o gwmpas yr Wyddfa, a Phen-y-Gwryd oedd eu cartref.[[cite:everest-pyg,pyg-wiki]] Ar 29 Mai 1953 safodd Edmund Hillary a Tenzing Norgay ar gopa’r byd.",
  "Nid anghofiasant y gwesty. Yn ei ystafell fwyta fach, llofnododd aelodau tîm Everest, a thîm alldaith 1955 a ddringodd Kangchenjunga gyntaf, eu henwau ar y nenfwd: Hillary, Tenzing a Hunt yn eu plith. Mae’r llofnodion yno o hyd, a dychwelodd y timau i aduniadau am flynyddoedd lawer.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
  "## Tafarn y dringwyr",
  "Dechreuodd y tŷ fel ffermdy yn 1811 ac roedd yn dafarn erbyn 1840. Dan Henry ac Ann Owen, a’i cadwodd am bron i hanner can mlynedd hyd y 1890au, daeth yn dafarn dringwyr enwocaf Cymru. Ym mis Mai 1898 sefydlwyd y Climbers’ Club yma.[[cite:pyg-wiki]] Mae’r ystafelloedd gwely wedi’u henwi ar ôl copaon Eryri dros dair mil o droedfeddi, ac mae’r gwesty’n dal yn orsaf achub mynydd.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
  "## Yn hŷn na’r dafarn",
  "Y Rhufeiniaid oedd yma gyntaf. Ym mhen Dyffryn Mymbyr, ar y llwybr y mae’r ffordd yn dal i’w ddilyn, adeiladodd y fyddin wersyll ymdaith o tua phedwar hectar, digon mawr i ryw ddwy fil o filwyr, yn ystod rhyfel Agricola yn erbyn yr Ordofigiaid yn niwedd y 70au OC mae’n debyg. Rhed ei ragfur gogleddol o dan y gwesty.[[cite:pyg-wiki]]",
 ]),

 "lavernock": ([
  "From the cliff at Lavernock Point, between Penarth and Sully, Guglielmo Marconi sent wireless messages across the open sea to the island of Flat Holm in May 1897. They were the first wireless signals ever sent and received over open sea.[[cite:marconi-veterans,lavernock-wiki]] The first message, tapped out in Morse code, asked: ‘Are you ready?’",
  "|| Where | Lavernock Point, Vale of Glamorgan, on the Bristol Channel",
  "|| When | 10 to 13 May 1897",
  "|| Across | To Flat Holm, about three and a third miles (5.4 km) out to sea",
  "|| Who | Guglielmo Marconi, helped by George Kemp of the Post Office",
  "|| Backed by | William Preece of Caernarfon, engineer-in-chief of the Post Office",
  "|| Remembered | A bronze plaque at St Lawrence’s church, put up in 1948",
  "## A Welsh engineer and a young Italian",
  "The man who brought Marconi to the Bristol Channel was a Welshman. William Preece, born at Caernarfon in 1834, was engineer-in-chief of the General Post Office, and in 1892 he had himself sent messages through the air across the Bristol Channel between Penarth and Flat Holm, a distance of more than three miles.[[cite:dwb-preece]] When the young Italian inventor came to Britain with his wireless apparatus, Preece became one of his strongest supporters and won him the help of the Post Office.[[cite:preece-wiki]]",
  "## Four days in May",
  "Preece’s own apparatus was set up again on Flat Holm, and Marconi’s on the cliff at Lavernock, with George Kemp, a Post Office engineer from Cardiff, at his side. On 10 May Preece’s method worked; on 11 and 12 May Marconi’s failed. On the 13th they carried the apparatus down to the beach and added some twenty yards of wire to raise the aerial higher, and the signals came through clearly.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] The messages went out in Morse: ‘Are you ready?’ and ‘Can you hear me?’ The answer came back: ‘Yes, loud and clear.’[[cite:lavernock-wiki,marconi-veterans]]",
  "Marconi soon moved across the water to Brean Down in Somerset and sent signals nearly ten miles.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] Kemp later left the Post Office to work for Marconi’s company.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] Within five years Marconi’s signals were crossing the Atlantic.",
  "## Lavernock today",
  "A bronze plaque in the churchyard wall of St Lawrence’s church, unveiled by the Cardiff Rotary Club in 1948, remembers the first wireless signals over the sea.[[cite:lavernock-wiki,marconi-urban75]] Near by are the gun emplacements of Lavernock Battery, built in the 1860s to guard the approach to Cardiff, and Lavernock Point nature reserve, where migrating birds come ashore after crossing from the islands.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]]",
 ], [
  "O’r clogwyn ar Drwyn Larnog, rhwng Penarth a Sili, anfonodd Guglielmo Marconi negeseuon diwifr dros y môr agored i Ynys Echni ym mis Mai 1897. Dyma’r signalau diwifr cyntaf erioed i’w hanfon a’u derbyn dros fôr agored.[[cite:marconi-veterans,lavernock-wiki]] Gofynnodd y neges gyntaf, wedi’i thapio mewn cod Morse: ‘Are you ready?’",
  "|| Ble | Trwyn Larnog, Bro Morgannwg, ar Fôr Hafren",
  "|| Pryd | 10 i 13 Mai 1897",
  "|| Ar draws | I Ynys Echni, tua thair milltir a thraean (5.4 km) allan i’r môr",
  "|| Pwy | Guglielmo Marconi, gyda chymorth George Kemp o’r Swyddfa Bost",
  "|| Ei gefnogi gan | William Preece o Gaernarfon, prif beiriannydd y Swyddfa Bost",
  "|| Ei goffáu | Plac efydd yn eglwys Sant Lawrence, a godwyd yn 1948",
  "## Peiriannydd o Gymro ac Eidalwr ifanc",
  "Cymro oedd y gŵr a ddaeth â Marconi i Fôr Hafren. Roedd William Preece, a aned yng Nghaernarfon yn 1834, yn brif beiriannydd y Swyddfa Bost, ac yn 1892 roedd ef ei hun wedi anfon negeseuon drwy’r awyr ar draws Môr Hafren rhwng Penarth ac Ynys Echni, pellter o fwy na thair milltir.[[cite:dwb-preece]] Pan ddaeth y dyfeisiwr ifanc o’r Eidal i Brydain â’i offer diwifr, daeth Preece yn un o’i gefnogwyr cryfaf ac ennill iddo gymorth y Swyddfa Bost.[[cite:preece-wiki]]",
  "## Pedwar diwrnod ym mis Mai",
  "Gosodwyd offer Preece ei hun eto ar Ynys Echni, ac offer Marconi ar y clogwyn yn Larnog, gyda George Kemp, peiriannydd y Swyddfa Bost o Gaerdydd, wrth ei ochr. Ar 10 Mai gweithiodd dull Preece; ar 11 a 12 Mai methodd un Marconi. Ar y 13eg cariasant yr offer i lawr i’r traeth ac ychwanegu rhyw ugain llath o wifren i godi’r erial yn uwch, a daeth y signalau drwodd yn glir.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] Aeth y negeseuon allan mewn Morse: ‘Are you ready?’ a ‘Can you hear me?’ Daeth yr ateb yn ôl: ‘Yes, loud and clear.’[[cite:lavernock-wiki,marconi-veterans]]",
  "Cyn hir symudodd Marconi dros y dŵr i Brean Down yng Ngwlad yr Haf ac anfon signalau bron i ddeng milltir.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] Yn ddiweddarach gadawodd Kemp y Swyddfa Bost i weithio i gwmni Marconi.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]] O fewn pum mlynedd roedd signalau Marconi yn croesi Môr Iwerydd.",
  "## Larnog heddiw",
  "Mae plac efydd ym mur mynwent eglwys Sant Lawrence, a ddadorchuddiwyd gan Glwb Rotari Caerdydd yn 1948, yn coffáu’r signalau diwifr cyntaf dros y môr.[[cite:lavernock-wiki,marconi-urban75]] Gerllaw mae safleoedd gynnau Batri Larnog, a godwyd yn y 1860au i warchod y ffordd i Gaerdydd, a gwarchodfa natur Trwyn Larnog, lle daw adar mudol i’r lan ar ôl croesi o’r ynysoedd.[[cite:lavernock-wiki]]",
 ]),
}

